# Build context trim for `docker build -f docker/Dockerfile .` (root context, whole tree).
#
# docker/Dockerfile deliberately does `COPY . .` rather than naming pom.xml and src/: a file list
# has to be edited every time the repo gains top-level content the build needs, and forgetting is
# silent. This file is what makes that affordable — it drops everything the build does not read, so
# adding a top-level entry stays free and no stale output leaks in.

# `**/.git` rather than `.git`: src/main/webui is a submodule, so it carries a .git FILE of its own
# pointing back into ../../../.git/modules. Neither is readable from the build stage and neither is
# read by it.
**/.git
.gitignore

# Maven / Quarkus build output — regenerated inside the build stage, and ~86 MB of it here. Copying
# it in would also risk a stale target/ shadowing what the stage compiles.
**/target
**/target-ide

# Node's install output, and the one entry here that is about SIZE: a pnpm node_modules for an
# Angular 21 workspace is hundreds of megabytes of symlink farm, and nothing in the build stage
# reads it — the stage runs no install and no client build (see docker/Dockerfile).
#
# NOTE THE ASYMMETRY with the platform's Quinoa recipe, which excludes the client's dist/ as well:
# here dist/ is the PAYLOAD. It is the bundle the pipeline step built where the platform's npm
# registry is reachable, it is the only form of the SPA this image can be given, and excluding it
# would fail the build at Quinoa's build-dir check.
src/main/webui/node_modules
src/main/webui/.angular

# Quinoa's own toolchain install directory. The build stage creates its own; a host-installed one
# would be a Linux/glibc question we do not want to import from whatever built the context.
.quinoa

# Local env / IDE cruft
.env
.idea
.vscode
.sdkmanrc

# Docs and the docker/ dir itself: nothing in the build reads them, and keeping them out means
# editing this Dockerfile or a README does not bust the `COPY . .` layer.
*.md
docker
